  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "Dozens of trapped hydropower tunnel workers in Nepal could still be alive, official says",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/sep/03/nepal-floods-rasuwagadhi-hydropower-hydro-tunnel-trapped-workers-search-rescue",
      "description": "<p>About 46 people believed to be trapped in ‘room-like’ structure inside hydro plant tunnel on Nepal-Tibet border</p><ul><li><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/sep/02/after-the-flood-human-suffering-nepal-worst-hit-districts\">After the flood: human suffering on an unfathomable scale in Nepal’s worst-hit districts</a></p></li></ul><p>Nepal authorities have said they are “confident” that dozens of workers in Nepal’s Rasuwagadhi hydropower plant are still alive, more than a week after deadly flash floods left them trapped in an underground chamber.</p><p>Authorities said that at least 639 workers were still missing from hydropower plants built along Nepal’s Bhote Koshi and Triushuli rivers.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/sep/03/nepal-floods-rasuwagadhi-hydropower-hydro-tunnel-trapped-workers-search-rescue\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-09-03T11:25:06"
    },
    {
      "title": "Aid operations in aftermath of deadly Nepal floods hampered by conditions | First Thing",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/sep/03/first-thing-aid-operations-deadly-nepal-floods-hampered-conditions",
      "description": "<p>Guardian reporters in Nepal have witnessed first-hand the scenes of destruction and grief after the flash floods. Plus, a not-to-be-missed interview with the legendary Mr T</p><p>Good morning. Thousands remain missing and the delivery of aid has been hampered by treacherous conditions in communities almost wiped out of existence by <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/sep/02/after-the-flood-human-suffering-nepal-worst-hit-districts\">the flash flooding in Nepal and Tibet</a>. A week after the disaster, survivors who fled up mountainsides are still being collected by army helicopters and evacuated.</p><p>The mountain village of Syabru Besi was among those destroyed, with even the road to the village gone. According to local government officials, at least 500 people there are missing, a quarter of the population. The village resembles nothing more than a muddy riverbed, with just one building left standing.</p><p><strong>What have Guardian reporters witnessed?</strong> Hannah Ellis-Petersen and Gaurav Pokharel have been in Betrawati and Rasuwa districts with the photographer Ahmer Khan. In this video, <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/video/2026/aug/31/on-the-ground-with-nepali-families-returning-to-what-remains-of-their-homes-video\">Ellis-Petersen explains what she has seen</a> alongside haunting footage of the debris. They have <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/sep/02/after-the-flood-human-suffering-nepal-worst-hit-districts\">spoken to multiple survivors</a> in a region now full of human suffering.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/sep/03/first-thing-aid-operations-deadly-nepal-floods-hampered-conditions\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-09-03T15:03:39"
    }
